[
{ch:10,q:"What does the periodic law state?",choices:["At regular but varying intervals, the chemical elements show an approximate repetition of their properties","Every element has exactly the same chemical properties as the element eight places after it","Atomic mass increases by exactly one unit between every pair of adjacent elements","All elements in the same period react identically with water"],explain:"The periodic law states that, at regular but varying intervals, the chemical elements show an approximate repetition of their properties, later explained by the periodic repetition of electron configurations."},
{ch:10,q:"In the modern periodic table, elements are arranged in order of increasing which quantity?",choices:["Atomic number (number of protons)","Relative atomic mass","Number of neutrons","Melting point"],explain:"Mendeleev originally used relative atomic mass, but the modern table arranges elements in order of increasing atomic number, Z, the number of protons in the nucleus."},
{ch:10,q:"What does the period number of an element tell you?",choices:["The number of occupied principal electron energy levels (shells) in its atoms","The number of valence electrons in its atoms","The number of protons in its nucleus","The number of naturally occurring isotopes of the element"],explain:"A period is a horizontal row, and the period number equals the number of occupied principal energy levels (shells) in a ground-state atom of that element."},
{ch:10,q:"Which elements make up the metalloids, sitting directly on the diagonal staircase that divides metals from non-metals?",choices:["Boron, silicon, germanium, arsenic, antimony, tellurium and polonium","Lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, caesium and francium","Fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine and astatine","Scandium, titanium, vanadium, chromium and manganese"],explain:"The metalloids sit along the staircase, looking physically like metals but reacting chemically more like non-metals; several, such as silicon and germanium, are semiconductors."},
{ch:10,q:"Which block of the periodic table is filled last by electrons entering a d sublevel, and what name is given to these elements?",choices:["The d block; they are called transition elements","The s block; they are called alkali metals","The p block; they are called halogens","The f block; they are called lanthanides"],explain:"The d block (groups 3-12) is filled by electrons entering d orbitals, and these elements, such as scandium to zinc in period 4, are called transition elements."},
{ch:10,q:"How many electrons are in the valence energy level of iodine, [Kr]4d<sup>10</sup>5s<sup>2</sup>5p<sup>5</sup>, and what does this confirm about its group?",choices:["7, confirming it is in group 17, the halogens","5, confirming it is in group 5","10, confirming it is in group 10","17, confirming it is in group 17 directly from the electron count"],explain:"Counting only the outer n = 5 electrons, 5s<sup>2</sup>5p<sup>5</sup>, gives 2 + 5 = 7 valence electrons, confirming iodine is a halogen in group 17."},
{ch:10,q:"The element with atomic number 20 has the configuration [Ar]4s<sup>2</sup>. Which period and group is it in, and which element is it?",choices:["Period 4, group 2 &mdash; calcium","Period 3, group 2 &mdash; magnesium","Period 4, group 18 &mdash; krypton","Period 2, group 4 &mdash; beryllium"],explain:"The highest occupied shell is n = 4 (period 4), and there are 2 valence electrons in an s sublevel (group 2), identifying the element as calcium."},
{ch:10,q:"How are the terms 'period' and 'group' best distinguished?",choices:["A period is a row, whose number gives the number of occupied shells; a group is a column, whose number is linked to the number of valence electrons","A period is a column and a group is a row, with the opposite meanings","Both terms mean exactly the same thing: a horizontal row of the table","A period refers only to the d block, while a group refers only to the s and p blocks"],explain:"A period is a horizontal row (number = occupied shells); a group is a vertical column (number linked to valence electrons and hence chemical behaviour)."},
{ch:10,q:"What happens to atomic radius down a group, and why?",choices:["Atomic radius increases, because each successive element has one more occupied shell, placing its outer electrons further from the nucleus","Atomic radius decreases, because nuclear charge increases faster than shielding","Atomic radius stays exactly constant, since effective nuclear charge is unchanged","Atomic radius increases only in groups 1 and 2, but decreases in all other groups"],explain:"Down a group, each successive element has one more occupied principal energy level, increasing atomic radius even though nuclear charge also increases."},
{ch:10,q:"What happens to atomic radius across a period, and why?",choices:["Atomic radius decreases, because increasing nuclear charge pulls the outer electrons (in the same shell) progressively closer to the nucleus","Atomic radius increases, because more electrons are being added to the atom","Atomic radius stays constant, since the number of occupied shells does not change","Atomic radius decreases only for metals, but increases for non-metals"],explain:"All elements in a period share the same number of occupied shells, but nuclear charge rises steadily left to right, decreasing atomic radius."},
{ch:10,q:"Why is a positive ion (cation) always smaller than its parent atom?",choices:["Forming a cation removes the entire outer occupied shell (or empties it substantially), so the remaining electrons are held by a higher effective charge per electron","Removing an electron always adds an extra occupied shell","Cations always have fewer protons than their parent atom","Positive ions repel each other more than neutral atoms do"],explain:"Forming a cation removes electrons from the outer shell, so the remaining electrons experience a higher effective nuclear charge per electron, shrinking the ion."},
{ch:10,q:"Why is a negative ion (anion) always larger than its parent atom?",choices:["Extra electrons added to the same outer shell increase electron&ndash;electron repulsion, forcing the electron cloud to expand","Anions always gain an extra occupied shell compared with the parent atom","Anions have fewer protons than their parent atom","Adding electrons always reduces the nuclear charge"],explain:"Adding electrons to the same outer shell increases electron-electron repulsion without adding any extra protons, expanding the ion."},
{ch:10,q:"O<sup>2&minus;</sup>, F<sup>&minus;</sup>, Ne, Na<sup>+</sup> and Mg<sup>2+</sup> are isoelectronic (all 1s<sup>2</sup>2s<sup>2</sup>2p<sup>6</sup>). Which is the smallest, and why?",choices:["Mg<sup>2+</sup>, because it has the largest nuclear charge (+12) pulling on the same fixed number of electrons","O<sup>2&minus;</sup>, because oxygen has the fewest protons","Ne, because neutral atoms are always smaller than ions","F<sup>&minus;</sup>, because fluorine is the most electronegative element"],explain:"All five species have 10 electrons, but nuclear charge increases steadily from O to Mg, so Mg<sup>2+</sup>, with the largest nuclear charge, is the smallest."},
{ch:10,q:"Why is there a large jump in ionic radius between Si<sup>4+</sup> (about 40 pm) and Si<sup>4&minus;</sup> (about 271 pm)?",choices:["Si<sup>4+</sup> has lost its entire outer (n = 3) shell and is measured by its smaller n = 2 shell, while Si<sup>4&minus;</sup> still has a filled, larger n = 3 shell","Si<sup>4+</sup> and Si<sup>4&minus;</sup> should actually be the same size, so this is an anomaly","Si<sup>4&minus;</sup> has fewer electrons than Si<sup>4+</sup>","The jump is caused only by a difference in nuclear charge, with no change in the number of occupied shells"],explain:"The large discontinuity arises because the cation has only two occupied shells while the anion still has three; it is the difference in occupied shells, not nuclear charge alone, that explains it."},
{ch:10,q:"How is the first ionization energy of an element defined?",choices:["The energy required to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms in their ground state, X(g) &rarr; X<sup>+</sup>(g) + e<sup>&minus;</sup>","The energy released when one mole of gaseous atoms gains one mole of electrons","The energy required to remove all the electrons from one atom","The energy released when one mole of a solid element melts"],explain:"First ionization energy is the energy required to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous ground-state atoms, forming 1+ ions; it is always endothermic."},
{ch:10,q:"What happens to first ionization energy down a group, and why?",choices:["It decreases, because the outer electron is further from the nucleus while effective nuclear charge stays roughly constant, weakening the attraction","It increases, because nuclear charge increases faster than atomic radius","It stays exactly constant across every group","It decreases only in group 1, but increases in every other group"],explain:"Down a group, the electron removed is further from the nucleus, and effective nuclear charge stays roughly constant, so the weaker attraction lowers ionization energy."},
{ch:10,q:"What happens to first ionization energy across a period, and why?",choices:["It generally increases, because effective nuclear charge rises steadily, holding the outer electrons more tightly","It generally decreases, because more electrons are added to the same shell","It stays constant, since the number of occupied shells does not change","It increases only for non-metals, but decreases for metals"],explain:"Across a period, effective nuclear charge rises steadily, so outer electrons are held more tightly and become harder to remove."},
{ch:10,q:"Why does first ionization energy drop slightly between beryllium (group 2) and boron (group 13)?",choices:["Boron's outer electron is removed from a 2p orbital, which is at a slightly higher energy than the 2s orbital from which beryllium's outer electron is removed","Boron has fewer protons than beryllium","Beryllium's outer electron occupies a p orbital while boron's occupies an s orbital","Boron's atomic radius is larger than beryllium's, which always lowers ionization energy regardless of sublevel"],explain:"Beryllium's outer electron is removed from the 2s sublevel, but boron's is removed from the slightly higher-energy 2p sublevel, making it easier to remove despite boron's greater nuclear charge."},
{ch:10,q:"Why does first ionization energy drop slightly between nitrogen (group 15) and oxygen (group 16)?",choices:["Oxygen's fourth 2p electron must pair up in an already-occupied orbital, and the extra electron&ndash;electron repulsion makes it easier to remove than expected from nuclear charge alone","Nitrogen has a higher nuclear charge than oxygen","Oxygen's outer electron occupies a 3p orbital, unlike nitrogen's 2p electron","Nitrogen's three 2p electrons are all paired in one orbital, unlike oxygen's"],explain:"Nitrogen's three 2p electrons each occupy a separate orbital, but oxygen's fourth electron must pair up, and the added repulsion makes it slightly easier to remove, causing the dip."},
{ch:10,q:"How is the first electron affinity of an element defined, and is the process generally exothermic or endothermic?",choices:["The energy change when one mole of electrons is added to one mole of gaseous atoms to form 1&minus; ions; generally exothermic","The energy required to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms; always endothermic","The energy released when a solid element dissolves in water; always exothermic","The energy change when two gaseous atoms bond covalently; always endothermic"],explain:"Electron affinity, X(g) + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; X<sup>&minus;</sup>(g), is generally exothermic because the added electron is attracted to the positively charged nucleus."},
{ch:10,q:"Why are the noble gases not assigned electron affinity values?",choices:["They do not generally form stable negatively charged ions","Their electron affinity is always exactly zero by definition","They have the highest electron affinities of any group, so values are omitted for simplicity","Electron affinity only applies to metals, and noble gases are non-metals"],explain:"The noble gases already have a complete outer shell and do not generally form stable negative ions, so electron affinity values are not assigned to them."},
{ch:10,q:"Which statement correctly describes electronegativity, and which element has the highest value?",choices:["It is a measure of an atom's ability to attract bonding electrons in a covalent bond, and cannot be measured for an isolated atom; fluorine has the highest value (4.0)","It is a directly measurable property of an isolated gaseous atom, just like ionization energy; caesium has the highest value","It applies equally to all elements, including the noble gases; oxygen has the highest value","It decreases across a period and increases down a group; francium has the highest value"],explain:"Electronegativity describes an atom's pulling power on electrons already shared in a covalent bond; it increases across a period and decreases down a group, and fluorine has the highest value of any element, 4.0 on the Pauling scale."},
{ch:10,q:"What happens to melting point down group 1, and why?",choices:["It decreases, because the delocalized outer electrons sit further from the ion cores as atoms get larger, weakening the metallic bond","It increases, because the metallic bond gets stronger with more shells","It stays exactly constant down the whole group","It decreases only between lithium and sodium, but then increases"],explain:"Down group 1, melting point falls from lithium (181&deg;C) to caesium (28&deg;C) because the delocalized electrons sit further from the ion cores in larger atoms, weakening the metallic bond."},
{ch:10,q:"What happens to melting point down group 17, and why?",choices:["It increases, because the molecules get larger and more polarizable, strengthening the London (van der Waals) forces between them","It decreases, because covalent bonds within each molecule get weaker","It stays constant, since all halogens are simple molecular substances","It decreases, because atomic radius decreases down the group"],explain:"Down group 17, melting point rises from fluorine (a gas) to iodine (a solid) as the molecules become larger and more polarizable, strengthening the London forces between molecules."},
{ch:10,q:"How does melting point change across period 3, from sodium to argon?",choices:["It rises through the metals (Na &lt; Mg &lt; Al), peaks sharply at silicon (a giant covalent structure), then falls to low values for the simple molecular non-metals and argon","It falls steadily and smoothly from sodium to argon with no peak","It rises steadily and smoothly from sodium to argon with no peak","It is the same for every period 3 element, since they are all in the same period"],explain:"Melting point climbs through the three metals, peaks sharply at silicon's giant covalent network, then collapses for the molecular elements P, S and Cl, reaching its lowest point at argon."},
{ch:10,q:"Why are the noble gases the least reactive elements in the periodic table?",choices:["Their atoms already have a complete outer energy level, so there is no thermodynamic driving force to lose or gain electrons","They have no electrons in their outer shell at all","They are the smallest atoms in the periodic table","They have the highest electronegativity of any group"],explain:"The noble gases have a complete outer energy level, so there is no driving force to lose or gain electrons; however, xenon was made to react with fluorine as early as 1962."},
{ch:10,q:"How does the reactivity of the alkali metals (group 1) change down the group, and why?",choices:["Reactivity increases down the group, because first ionization energy decreases, so the single valence electron is lost more readily","Reactivity decreases down the group, because atomic radius decreases","Reactivity stays constant down the group","Reactivity increases down the group because electronegativity increases"],explain:"Reactivity of the alkali metals increases down the group because first ionization energy decreases, so the outer electron is lost more readily from lithium down to francium."},
{ch:10,q:"Lithium, sodium and potassium are each dropped into separate beakers of water. What is observed as you move down the group, and why?",choices:["The reaction becomes more vigorous (lithium floats and fizzes gently, sodium melts into a moving ball, potassium ignites the hydrogen produced), because the metals become more reactive down the group","The reaction becomes less vigorous down the group, because reactivity decreases from lithium to potassium","All three metals react identically, releasing exactly the same amount of heat","None of the three metals reacts with cold water"],explain:"Reactivity increases down group 1, so the reaction becomes visibly more vigorous from lithium (gentle fizzing) to sodium (melts, moves) to potassium (ignites the hydrogen, lilac flame)."},
{ch:10,q:"How does the reactivity of the halogens (group 17), as oxidizing agents, change down the group, and why?",choices:["Reactivity decreases down the group, because the increasing atomic radius places an incoming electron further from the nucleus, weakening the attraction","Reactivity increases down the group, because atomic radius increases","Reactivity stays constant down the group","Reactivity decreases down the group because ionization energy decreases"],explain:"Reactivity of the halogens as oxidizing agents decreases down the group: fluorine's small radius gives the strongest pull on an incoming electron, weakening further down the group."},
{ch:10,q:"Chlorine water is added to a colourless solution of potassium bromide. What is observed, and why?",choices:["The solution turns orange, as chlorine (a more reactive, more strongly oxidizing halogen) displaces bromine from the bromide ions: Cl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + 2Br<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; 2Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + Br<sub>2</sub>(aq)","No visible reaction occurs, since chlorine cannot displace bromine","The solution turns violet, as iodine is produced","The solution turns white, as a precipitate of KCl forms"],explain:"Chlorine is above bromine in group 17, so it is more strongly oxidizing; it takes an electron from each bromide ion, producing orange Br<sub>2</sub>(aq)."},
{ch:10,q:"What colours are the precipitates formed when silver ions react with chloride, bromide and iodide ions respectively?",choices:["Silver chloride is white, silver bromide is cream/pale yellow, and silver iodide is a distinctly darker yellow","All three silver halide precipitates are exactly the same shade of white","Silver chloride is yellow, silver bromide is white, and silver iodide is cream","Silver halides do not form precipitates; they all remain fully dissolved"],explain:"Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) + X<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; AgX(s) gives white (chloride), cream/pale yellow (bromide), and distinctly darker yellow (iodide) precipitates."},
{ch:10,q:"What is the relationship between a period 3 element's group number and the maximum oxidation state it shows in its oxide?",choices:["The maximum oxidation state equals the group number (using the 1&ndash;18 numbering), since the atom uses all its valence electrons to bond with the highly electronegative oxygen","The maximum oxidation state is always +2, regardless of group number","The maximum oxidation state decreases as group number increases","There is no relationship between group number and oxidation state in oxides"],explain:"The maximum oxidation state in a period 3 oxide equals its group number (up to +7 for chlorine), since the atom uses all of its valence electrons to bond with the highly electronegative oxygen."},
{ch:10,q:"The high conductivity of molten Na<sub>2</sub>O, MgO and Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, compared with the total absence of conductivity in molten P<sub>4</sub>O<sub>10</sub>, SO<sub>3</sub> and Cl<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>, provides evidence for what?",choices:["The first three oxides are ionic lattices with mobile ions when molten, while the latter three are simple covalent molecules with no free ions","All six oxides are actually covalent, with no ionic character at all","The latter three oxides are more ionic than the first three","Conductivity in the molten state gives no information about bonding type"],explain:"An oxide only conducts electricity in the molten state if charged ions are free to move, so this evidence directly confirms the ionic lattices of Na<sub>2</sub>O, MgO and Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> and the covalent molecular nature of the others."},
{ch:10,q:"How does the acid&ndash;base character of the period 3 oxides change from sodium to chlorine?",choices:["It shifts from strongly basic (Na<sub>2</sub>O, MgO), through amphoteric (Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>), to strongly acidic (P<sub>4</sub>O<sub>10</sub>, SO<sub>3</sub>, Cl<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>)","It stays neutral for every period 3 oxide","It shifts from strongly acidic to strongly basic from left to right","It is unrelated to the bonding character of each oxide"],explain:"Ionic, metallic oxides are generally basic, oxides with mixed character can be amphoteric, and covalent, non-metallic oxides are generally acidic, mirroring the change from ionic to covalent bonding."},
{ch:10,q:"Aluminium oxide reacts with a strong acid: Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s) + 6H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 2Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + 3H<sub>2</sub>O(l). Together with its reaction with a strong alkali, what property does this demonstrate?",choices:["Amphoteric character &mdash; it can react as both a base (with an acid) and an acid (with an alkali)","Purely acidic character only","Purely basic character only","That aluminium oxide is completely unreactive"],explain:"Aluminium oxide reacts as a base towards an acid and as an acid towards an alkali, Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s) + 3H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + 2OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; 2Al(OH)<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), demonstrating amphoteric character."},
{ch:10,q:"Sulfur dioxide, released mainly from burning fossil fuels and ore smelting, contributes to acid rain mainly by which process?",choices:["Dissolving in rainwater directly to form sulfuric(IV) acid, or being oxidized in the atmosphere to SO<sub>3</sub> which forms the stronger sulfuric(VI) acid","Reacting directly with nitrogen in the air to form nitric acid","Dissolving in rainwater with no acid formed at all","Converting carbon dioxide in the atmosphere into carbonic acid"],explain:"SO<sub>2</sub> dissolves to give H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>, or is oxidized to SO<sub>3</sub>, 2SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2SO<sub>3</sub>(g), which forms the much stronger H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> on dissolving."},
{ch:10,q:"About half of the carbon dioxide released by burning fossil fuels dissolves into the oceans. What is the main consequence of this for marine life?",choices:["The resulting rise in ocean acidity inhibits shell and skeleton growth in many marine organisms","It has no measurable chemical effect on seawater","It makes the oceans more alkaline, which strengthens marine shells","It causes seawater to lose all of its dissolved oxygen"],explain:"Dissolved CO<sub>2</sub> forms carbonic acid, which partly dissociates, raising ocean surface acidity and inhibiting shell and skeleton growth in many marine organisms."},
{ch:10,q:"Which statement correctly defines the oxidation state (oxidation number) of an atom?",choices:["The charge that atom would have if the compound it is part of were composed entirely of ions","The actual, measured electrical charge on that atom within a molecule","The total number of electrons in that atom's outer shell","The number of covalent bonds that atom forms in the compound"],explain:"Oxidation state is a book-keeping tool: the charge an atom would have if the compound were composed entirely of ions, regardless of whether the actual bonding is ionic or covalent."},
{ch:10,q:"What is the oxidation state of sulfur in H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>?",choices:["+6","+4","+2","+8"],explain:"H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> is neutral: 2(+1) + S + 4(&minus;2) = 0, so S = +6."},
{ch:10,q:"What is the oxidation state of sulfur in the sulfite ion, SO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>?",choices:["+4","+6","+2","&minus;2"],explain:"SO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> sums to its charge, &minus;2: S + 3(&minus;2) = &minus;2, so S = +4."},
{ch:10,q:"What is the oxidation state of oxygen in hydrogen peroxide, H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, and why is it an exception to the usual rule?",choices:["&minus;1, because the O&ndash;O bond in a peroxide splits the usual electron count between the two oxygen atoms","&minus;2, the same as in every other oxygen-containing compound","+2, because hydrogen is less electronegative than oxygen","0, because oxygen is present as a free element in H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>"],explain:"Oxygen is usually &minus;2, but in peroxides such as H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> it is &minus;1, since the O&ndash;O bond splits the usual electron count between the two oxygens."},
{ch:10,q:"What is the oxidation state of hydrogen in sodium hydride, NaH, and why is it an exception to the usual rule?",choices:["&minus;1, because hydrogen is less electronegative than the group 1 metal, so hydrogen gains control of the shared electrons","+1, the same as in every other hydrogen-containing compound","0, because hydrogen is present as a free element in NaH","+2, because sodium is more electronegative than hydrogen"],explain:"Hydrogen is usually +1, but in metal hydrides such as NaH it is &minus;1, because hydrogen is less electronegative than a group 1 or 2 metal."},
{ch:10,q:"Which statement correctly defines a disproportionation reaction?",choices:["A redox reaction in which atoms of the same element, starting in a single oxidation state, end up simultaneously oxidized and reduced into two different oxidation states in the products","A reaction in which no element changes oxidation state at all","A reaction in which a single element is only ever oxidized, never reduced","A reaction that can only occur between two different elements, never within one"],explain:"Disproportionation is a redox reaction in which atoms of one element, starting at a single oxidation state, end up simultaneously oxidized and reduced in the products."},
{ch:10,q:"In H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; HNO<sub>2</sub>(aq) + HNO<sub>3</sub>(aq), nitrogen starts at +4 in NO<sub>2</sub>. What happens to its oxidation state, and what type of reaction is this?",choices:["Nitrogen ends up at both +3 (in HNO<sub>2</sub>, reduced) and +5 (in HNO<sub>3</sub>, oxidized) &mdash; a disproportionation reaction","Nitrogen ends up at +4 in both products, so no redox change occurs","Nitrogen is only oxidized, ending at +5 in both products","Nitrogen is only reduced, ending at +3 in both products"],explain:"Nitrogen is +3 in HNO<sub>2</sub> and +5 in HNO<sub>3</sub>; starting from a single state (+4) and ending up both oxidized and reduced makes this a disproportionation reaction."},
{ch:10,q:"Using IUPAC oxidation-number nomenclature, what is the systematic name of H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (where S = +6)?",choices:["Sulfuric(VI) acid","Sulfuric(IV) acid","Sulfuric(II) acid","Sulfuric(VIII) acid"],explain:"Since sulfur is +6 in H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, its systematic name is sulfuric(VI) acid, distinguishing it from sulfuric(IV) acid, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What is the defining feature of a transition element?",choices:["Its atoms have an incomplete d sublevel, or can give rise to a cation with an incomplete d sublevel","Its atoms have a complete d sublevel in every compound it forms","It belongs to groups 1 or 2 of the periodic table","It only ever exists in a single oxidation state"],explain:"A transition element is a d-block element whose atoms have an incomplete d sublevel, or which forms at least one cation with an incomplete d sublevel."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Which electron configurations correctly show the anomalous first-row d-block elements chromium and copper?",choices:["Cr is [Ar]3d<sup>5</sup>4s<sup>1</sup> and Cu is [Ar]3d<sup>10</sup>4s<sup>1</sup>, rather than the 3d<sup>4</sup>4s<sup>2</sup> and 3d<sup>9</sup>4s<sup>2</sup> expected from a simple filling order","Cr is [Ar]3d<sup>4</sup>4s<sup>2</sup> and Cu is [Ar]3d<sup>9</sup>4s<sup>2</sup>, following the expected filling order exactly","Both Cr and Cu have a completely full 4s sublevel and an empty 3d sublevel","Cr and Cu are identical to scandium and titanium in their electron configurations"],explain:"A half-filled (Cr, 3d<sup>5</sup>) or completely filled (Cu, 3d<sup>10</sup>) d sublevel together with a single 4s electron is energetically favourable, making these two elements exceptions to the simple filling order."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why is there only a small variation in atomic radius and first ionization energy across the first-row transition elements, compared with a typical s- or p-block period?",choices:["The increase in nuclear charge across the row is largely offset by the extra shielding from each newly added inner 3d electron, so effective nuclear charge rises only slightly","The transition elements all have exactly the same number of protons","Nuclear charge does not increase at all across the d block","The 4s electrons are removed before the 3d electrons fill, cancelling any change in radius"],explain:"Because the d electrons being added are in an inner sublevel, they shield the outer 4s electrons almost as effectively as the extra proton attracts them, so effective nuclear charge &mdash; and radius and ionization energy &mdash; change only slightly across the row."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why does the relatively small variation in atomic radius across the d block make it easy for these metals to form alloys with one another?",choices:["Atoms of one d-block metal can replace atoms of another in the lattice without much disruption of the solid structure, since the atoms are all a similar size","Alloys can only form between elements with very different atomic radii","d-block metals cannot form alloys with each other under any circumstances","Atomic radius has no influence on whether two metals can form an alloy"],explain:"Because d-block atomic radii are all fairly similar, one metal's atoms can substitute for another's within a metallic lattice without severely disrupting the regular packing, allowing ready alloy formation."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why is zinc not classified as a transition element, even though it is in the d block?",choices:["Both zinc metal, [Ar]3d<sup>10</sup>4s<sup>2</sup>, and its only ion, Zn<sup>2+</sup> ([Ar]3d<sup>10</sup>), have a completely full d sublevel in every compound zinc forms","Zinc does not belong to the d block at all","Zinc has an incomplete d sublevel in both the metal and its ion, just like scandium","Zinc forms a wide range of coloured compounds, unlike true transition elements"],explain:"Because zinc's d sublevel is always completely full (3d<sup>10</sup>), it never shows the variable oxidation states or coloured compounds characteristic of true transition elements."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why is scandium considered a borderline case for transition-element status?",choices:["Its most common ion, Sc<sup>3+</sup>, has a completely empty d sublevel ([Ar]), so like zinc it forms no coloured compounds, even though the rarer Sc<sup>2+</sup> does retain one d electron","Scandium always shows a full d sublevel in every oxidation state it forms","Scandium is actually an s-block element, not a d-block element","Scandium forms only covalent compounds, never ionic ones"],explain:"Sc<sup>3+</sup> ([Ar], 3d<sup>0</sup>) has an empty d sublevel, so scandium (like zinc, at the opposite end) forms no coloured compounds in its most common oxidation state, making its classification as a transition element debatable."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why does calcium show only the +2 oxidation state, with no evidence of a stable Ca<sup>3+</sup> ion?",choices:["Removing a third electron would require breaking into the full, lower-energy 3p sublevel of the noble-gas core, requiring a huge jump in ionization energy","Calcium has no electrons available to remove beyond the second ionization","Calcium's third ionization energy is actually lower than its second","Calcium only has one electron in its outer shell"],explain:"After removing the two 4s electrons, a third ionization would remove an electron from the full, much lower-energy 3p sublevel, requiring a very large jump in energy, so Ca<sup>3+</sup> is never formed."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why does vanadium show the +2, +3, +4 and +5 oxidation states, with only a gradual increase in successive ionization energies up to the fifth electron removed?",choices:["The 3d and 4s sublevels in vanadium are close in energy, so each successive ionization up to the fifth electron requires only a gradually increasing amount of energy, with no sharp jump until the inner 3p sublevel is reached","Vanadium has no 3d electrons at all, only 4s electrons","All five of vanadium's outer electrons are removed with exactly the same amount of energy","Vanadium's electron configuration contains no inner shells at all"],explain:"Because 3d and 4s are close in energy in vanadium, [Ar]3d<sup>3</sup>4s<sup>2</sup>, the first five ionizations proceed with only gradually increasing energy, giving oxidation states up to +5, with a sharp jump only at the sixth (an inner 3p electron)."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Across the first transition series, how does the maximum oxidation state generally change from scandium to zinc?",choices:["It increases in steps of +1, reaching a maximum of +7 at manganese, then decreases in steps of &minus;1 for the later elements","It increases steadily all the way from scandium (+1) to zinc (+12)","It decreases steadily from scandium to zinc with no maximum","It stays constant at +2 for every element in the series"],explain:"The maximum oxidation state rises in steps of +1 to a peak of +7 at manganese (using both its 4s and all five 3d electrons, as in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>), then falls in steps of &minus;1 as nuclear charge increases further along the row."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why do transition metal compounds with oxidation states above +3 tend to show more covalent character and act as strong oxidizing agents?",choices:["A small, highly charged ion polarizes neighbouring negative ions strongly, increasing covalent character, and such high-oxidation-state species (e.g. dichromate(VI), permanganate(VII)) readily remove electrons from other species","High oxidation states always correspond to purely ionic bonding with no covalent character at all","Oxidation states above +3 are never found in real transition metal compounds","Covalent character and oxidizing strength are unrelated to oxidation state"],explain:"A highly charged, compact ion has a large charge density, which polarizes nearby anions and increases covalent character; such species are also often powerful oxidizing agents, as seen with dichromate(VI) and permanganate(VII)."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What general physical properties do the transition metals share, and what is the main explanation for them?",choices:["High electrical/thermal conductivity, high melting point, hardness and malleability, explained by strong metallic bonding since both 3d and 4s electrons join the delocalized sea of electrons","Low melting points and poor electrical conductivity, because the d electrons are tightly localized and do not delocalize","High reactivity with water, releasing hydrogen gas, just like the alkali metals","Low density, because transition metal atoms are unusually large"],explain:"Because the 3d and 4s electrons are close in energy, both become part of the delocalized electron sea, giving unusually strong metallic bonding and hence high conductivity, high melting points and hardness."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why do transition metals typically have higher densities than their s-block neighbours in the same period?",choices:["Their smaller atomic radii, compared with the s-block elements in the same period, pack more mass into a similar volume","Transition metals always have fewer protons than s-block metals in the same period","Transition metals have larger atomic radii than s-block metals, which increases density","Density is unrelated to atomic radius or mass"],explain:"The transition metals have noticeably smaller atomic radii than the s-block metals in the same period, which, combined with their greater atomic mass, gives them characteristically high densities."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What causes the magnetic property called paramagnetism in a transition metal ion?",choices:["The presence of one or more unpaired electrons in its d orbitals, which are attracted into an external magnetic field","The presence of only paired electrons in its d orbitals","A completely empty d sublevel in the ion","The total number of protons in the metal's nucleus"],explain:"Paramagnetism arises from unpaired d electrons; paired electrons have opposite spins that cancel, giving no net magnetic effect, so only ions with unpaired d electrons are attracted into a magnetic field."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Which of the following ions would be expected to show no paramagnetism at all, and why?",choices:["Zn<sup>2+</sup> ([Ar]3d<sup>10</sup>), because its d sublevel is completely full, leaving no unpaired electrons","Mn<sup>2+</sup> ([Ar]3d<sup>5</sup>), because it has no d electrons at all","Fe<sup>3+</sup> ([Ar]3d<sup>5</sup>), because all five of its d electrons are paired","Cr<sup>3+</sup> ([Ar]3d<sup>3</sup>), because chromium is not a d-block element"],explain:"Zn<sup>2+</sup> has a completely full 3d<sup>10</sup> sublevel, so every electron is paired and there is no net magnetic effect, unlike Mn<sup>2+</sup> (3d<sup>5</sup>), which has five unpaired electrons and is strongly paramagnetic."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Iron, cobalt and nickel show an unusually strong form of magnetism in which neighbouring atoms' unpaired electron spins align cooperatively throughout the solid metal. What is this property called?",choices:["Ferromagnetism","Paramagnetism","Diamagnetism","Electronegativity"],explain:"Ferromagnetism is the cooperative alignment of unpaired electron spins between neighbouring atoms throughout a solid such as iron, cobalt or nickel, giving a much stronger magnetic effect than simple paramagnetism."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What is the definition of a catalyst, and how do transition metals commonly act in this role?",choices:["A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy; transition metals and their ions are effective catalysts because their variable oxidation states and available d orbitals let them form temporary bonds with reactants","A catalyst is a reactant that is permanently consumed during the reaction; transition metals are consumed when acting as catalysts","A catalyst always increases the activation energy of a reaction to slow it down","Transition metals can only catalyse reactions that involve other metals"],explain:"A catalyst speeds up a reaction by providing a lower-activation-energy pathway; transition metals are effective catalysts because their variable oxidation states and d orbitals allow temporary, reversible bonding with reactant species."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What is the key difference between a heterogeneous and a homogeneous catalyst?",choices:["A heterogeneous catalyst is in a different phase from the reactants, while a homogeneous catalyst is in the same phase as the reactants","A heterogeneous catalyst is always a gas, while a homogeneous catalyst is always a liquid","A heterogeneous catalyst speeds up a reaction, while a homogeneous catalyst slows it down","There is no meaningful difference between the two terms"],explain:"A heterogeneous catalyst exists in a different phase from the reactants (e.g. a solid catalysing a gas-phase reaction), while a homogeneous catalyst exists in the same phase, typically dissolved alongside the reactants."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Iron is used as a catalyst in the Haber process, N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &#8652; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g). Is this an example of heterogeneous or homogeneous catalysis, and why?",choices:["Heterogeneous, because the solid iron catalyst is in a different phase from the gaseous reactants","Homogeneous, because iron dissolves into the gas mixture during the reaction","Heterogeneous, because iron is itself a reactant that gets consumed","Homogeneous, because iron and the gases are all in the same phase"],explain:"Solid iron provides a surface on which the strong N&equiv;N triple bond can be weakened; since the solid catalyst is in a different phase from the gaseous reactants, this is heterogeneous catalysis."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Nickel is used to catalytically hydrogenate the carbon&ndash;carbon double bonds in unsaturated vegetable oils. What is the main industrial product of this process?",choices:["Margarine, from the resulting saturated fats","Petrol, from the resulting hydrocarbons","Ammonia, for use in fertilizers","Sulfuric acid, for use in industry"],explain:"Solid nickel catalyses the addition of hydrogen across carbon-carbon double bonds in unsaturated vegetable oils, converting them into the saturated fats used to make margarine."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: In a catalytic converter, platinum, rhodium and palladium catalyse the reaction 2CO(g) + 2NO(g) &rarr; 2CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N<sub>2</sub>(g). What is the purpose of this reaction?",choices:["Converting toxic carbon monoxide and nitrogen oxide exhaust gases into relatively harmless carbon dioxide and nitrogen","Producing ammonia for use as a fertilizer","Converting unsaturated vegetable oils into saturated fats","Removing sulfur dioxide from power-station flue gases"],explain:"The precious-metal catalysts in a catalytic converter convert the toxic primary pollutants CO and NO from vehicle exhaust into the far less harmful CO<sub>2</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Solid manganese(IV) oxide, MnO<sub>2</sub>, catalyses the decomposition of aqueous hydrogen peroxide, 2H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>(aq) &rarr; 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + O<sub>2</sub>(g). What type of catalysis is this, and why?",choices:["Heterogeneous, because the solid MnO<sub>2</sub> catalyst is in a different phase from the aqueous hydrogen peroxide","Homogeneous, because MnO<sub>2</sub> fully dissolves in the hydrogen peroxide solution","Heterogeneous, because MnO<sub>2</sub> is permanently consumed by the reaction","Homogeneous, because oxygen gas and MnO<sub>2</sub> are in the same phase"],explain:"Solid MnO<sub>2</sub> catalyses the decomposition of aqueous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> without being consumed; since the solid catalyst is in a different phase from the aqueous reactant, this is heterogeneous catalysis."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: When acidified potassium manganate(VII) reacts with oxalate ions, the reaction starts slowly but speeds up noticeably as it proceeds, even though reactant concentration is falling. What explains this, and what is the phenomenon called?",choices:["The Mn<sup>2+</sup> ions formed as a product catalyse the same reaction; a reaction catalysed by one of its own products is called autocatalysis","The reaction is actually slowing down throughout, and no unusual effect occurs","Oxalate ions act as a heterogeneous catalyst for their own oxidation","The reaction only speeds up because the temperature of the solution decreases over time"],explain:"Mn<sup>2+</sup>, produced as the reaction proceeds, catalyses the further reduction of MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> via the Mn<sup>3+</sup>/Mn<sup>2+</sup> couple; a reaction catalysed by its own product is called autocatalysis."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why is the autocatalysis of the permanganate/oxalate reaction described as homogeneous catalysis?",choices:["The catalytic Mn<sup>2+</sup> species is dissolved in the same aqueous phase as the reactants","The catalyst is a solid, while the reactants are in solution","The reaction takes place entirely in the gas phase","Homogeneous catalysis simply means the reaction has only one product"],explain:"Because the Mn<sup>2+</sup> catalyst produced during the reaction is dissolved in the same aqueous solution as the MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> and oxalate reactants, this is an example of homogeneous (same-phase) catalysis."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What is a ligand, and how does it bond to a central metal ion in a complex?",choices:["A molecule or ion that donates a lone pair of electrons to the metal ion, forming a coordination (dative covalent) bond","A molecule that always carries a full negative charge and bonds ionically to the metal","An atom that shares one unpaired electron with the metal ion in an ordinary covalent bond","A ligand is simply another name for the central metal ion itself"],explain:"A ligand is a molecule or ion with at least one lone pair of electrons, which it donates to the central metal ion to form a coordination (dative covalent) bond."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What does the coordination number of a complex ion describe?",choices:["The total number of coordination bonds formed between the ligands and the central metal ion","The total charge on the complex ion","The number of different types of ligand present in the complex","The oxidation state of the central metal ion"],explain:"The coordination number is the total number of coordination bonds from ligands to the central ion, such as 6 in an octahedral complex or 4 in a tetrahedral one."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: In the complex ion [Fe(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>, what is the coordination number of iron, and what geometry does this typically give?",choices:["6, giving an octahedral arrangement of ligands","4, giving a tetrahedral arrangement of ligands","2, giving a linear arrangement of ligands","6, giving a planar hexagonal arrangement of ligands"],explain:"Six water ligands each donate a lone pair to the central Fe<sup>2+</sup> ion, giving a coordination number of 6 and an octahedral geometry, the typical arrangement for small ligands such as H<sub>2</sub>O and NH<sub>3</sub>."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why does replacing water ligands with the larger chloride ligand, as in [CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup>, typically change the coordination number from 6 to 4?",choices:["Chloride ions are larger and more sterically demanding than water molecules, so fewer of them can fit around the central metal ion, giving a tetrahedral rather than octahedral geometry","Chloride ions cannot form coordination bonds at all, so the coordination number must decrease","Copper can only ever have a coordination number of 4, regardless of the ligand","Chloride ligands always increase the coordination number compared with water"],explain:"Because Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> ions are larger and more sterically demanding than H<sub>2</sub>O molecules, fewer can pack around the central metal ion, often giving a tetrahedral complex with coordination number 4 instead of an octahedral one with coordination number 6."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What is the correct systematic name for the complex ion [Fe(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>?",choices:["Hexaaquairon(II) ion","Hexaaquairon(III) ion","Hexaamminecobalt(II) ion","Tetraaquairon(II) ion"],explain:"The six water (aqua) ligands and iron's +2 oxidation state give the name hexaaquairon(II) ion: hexa- (6) + aqua (H<sub>2</sub>O) + iron(II)."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What is the correct systematic name for the negatively charged complex ion [CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup>?",choices:["Tetrachlorocuprate(II) ion","Tetrachlorocopper(II) ion","Tetraamminecuprate(II) ion","Dichlorocuprate(IV) ion"],explain:"For an overall negative complex, the metal name takes the suffix &ldquo;-ate&rdquo;: four chloro ligands and copper's +2 state give tetrachlorocuprate(II) ion."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What is the oxidation state of iron in the complex ion [Fe(CN)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>4&minus;</sup>?",choices:["+2","+3","+4","+6"],explain:"The six CN<sup>&minus;</sup> ligands and the overall &minus;4 charge give Fe + 6(&minus;1) = &minus;4, so Fe = +2."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: In an isolated gaseous transition metal ion, what is true of the five d orbitals?",choices:["They are degenerate, meaning they all have exactly the same energy","They already have two completely different energy levels, even without any ligands present","Only three of the five d orbitals actually exist in an isolated ion","They have five completely different energies, even in isolation"],explain:"In an isolated gaseous ion with no ligands present, the five d orbitals are degenerate &mdash; all at the same energy; splitting into two levels only occurs once ligands approach."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: In an octahedral complex, why do the five d orbitals split into two different energy levels when ligands approach?",choices:["Two of the d orbitals point directly along the axes toward the approaching ligands and are repelled more strongly (raised in energy), while the other three point between the axes and are repelled less (lowered in energy)","All five d orbitals are repelled by exactly the same amount, so no splitting actually occurs","The ligands physically remove two of the five d orbitals from the ion","Splitting only happens in tetrahedral complexes, never in octahedral ones"],explain:"In an octahedral field, two d orbitals point directly at the six incoming ligands and experience stronger repulsion (higher energy), while the other three point between the ligand directions and experience weaker repulsion (lower energy), producing the split."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What condition must be met for a transition metal complex to absorb a photon of visible light in a d&ndash;d electron transition?",choices:["The photon's energy, hf, must exactly match the energy gap, &Delta;E, between the lower and upper split d-orbital levels","The photon must have the same energy as the first ionization energy of the metal","Any photon of visible light will always be absorbed by any transition metal ion","The ligands must be removed entirely before any light can be absorbed"],explain:"A d-d transition only occurs when a photon's energy exactly matches the gap &Delta;E between the split d levels; for most transition metal complexes this corresponds to a photon in the visible region."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: A solution absorbs light in the green region of the spectrum. What complementary colour would the solution appear to be, and why?",choices:["Purple, because green and purple are complementary colours on the colour wheel, so the light transmitted is the colour remaining once green is removed from white light","Green, because the colour observed is always the same as the colour absorbed","Red, because red is the complementary colour of every other colour","Yellow, because yellow light is never absorbed by any coloured solution"],explain:"The colour observed is complementary to the colour absorbed; since green and purple sit opposite each other on the colour wheel, a solution absorbing green light appears purple."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Copper(II) sulfate solution appears blue/turquoise. Which colour of light does it absorb, and why?",choices:["Orange light, because orange and blue/turquoise are complementary colours on the colour wheel","Blue light, because the colour observed is always identical to the colour absorbed","Green light, because green is always the complementary colour of blue","Ultraviolet light, which is invisible and unrelated to the observed colour"],explain:"Copper(II) sulfate absorbs orange light; orange and blue/turquoise are complementary colours, so the light transmitted (and observed) appears blue/turquoise."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: In colorimetry, what relationship allows the concentration of a coloured transition metal complex to be determined?",choices:["The absorbance of the solution at a fixed wavelength is directly proportional to its concentration, so a calibration curve from known standards can be used to find an unknown concentration","Absorbance is completely unrelated to the concentration of a coloured species","Only the colour of a solution, not its absorbance, can be used to estimate concentration","Concentration can only be measured by evaporating the solution completely and weighing the solid residue"],explain:"Because absorbance at a fixed wavelength is directly proportional to concentration, a calibration curve built from several standard solutions of known concentration allows an unknown sample's concentration to be read off from its measured absorbance."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: [Cr(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup> (violet) and [Fe(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup> (yellow) have the same charge and the same ligand, yet different colours. What factor explains this?",choices:["The identity of the metal, and hence the number of d electrons present, is different (chromium vs iron), giving a different d-orbital splitting and absorption","The coordination number of the two complexes is different","The ligand field strength of water changes depending on which metal it is bonded to","The two complexes actually absorb exactly the same wavelength of light"],explain:"Even with the same +3 charge and the same water ligands, different metals (with different numbers of d electrons) produce different d-orbital splitting and therefore absorb different wavelengths, giving different colours."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: [Fe(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> is pale green while [Fe(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup> is yellow/pale brown. What factor explains this colour difference?",choices:["The oxidation state of the metal is different (+2 vs +3), changing the size of the d-orbital energy gap even though the metal and ligand are the same","The two complexes have different coordination numbers","The ligand in the two complexes is different","Oxidation state has no effect on the colour of a transition metal complex"],explain:"Although the metal and ligand are identical, the different oxidation states of iron (+2 vs +3) produce a different d-orbital splitting and therefore a different colour."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Adding excess aqueous ammonia to pale blue [Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> produces deep/royal blue [Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>. What explains this colour change?",choices:["Ammonia has a stronger ligand field than water, producing a larger d-orbital energy gap and shifting absorption to shorter-wavelength (yellow) light","Ammonia has a weaker ligand field than water, producing a smaller energy gap","The coordination number increases from 6 to 8 when ammonia replaces water","Ammonia does not actually act as a ligand in this reaction"],explain:"Ammonia is a stronger-field ligand than water, producing a larger &Delta;E; this shifts the absorbed light to shorter wavelengths (yellow), giving the complex its deep blue appearance."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Write the equation for the ligand substitution reaction when excess concentrated hydrochloric acid is added to aqueous [Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>, producing [CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup>.",choices:["[Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; [CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","[Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 6Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; [CuCl<sub>6</sub>]<sup>4&minus;</sup>(aq) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","[Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; CuCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","[CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; [Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq)"],explain:"All six water ligands are substituted by four larger chloride ligands, changing both the coordination number (6 to 4) and geometry (octahedral to tetrahedral): [Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; [CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O(l)."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why does the colour change from pale blue [Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> to yellow-green [CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2&minus;</sup> involve more than just a change of ligand?",choices:["The coordination number and geometry also change (octahedral, 6 ligands, to tetrahedral, 4 ligands), producing a different d-orbital splitting pattern altogether","Copper's oxidation state changes from +2 to +3 during the substitution","No actual chemical change occurs; the colour change is purely due to dilution","The central metal ion itself changes from copper to a different element"],explain:"Because chloride ligands are larger, the complex adopts a different geometry (tetrahedral instead of octahedral) and coordination number, which changes the d-orbital splitting pattern itself, not just its magnitude."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Cobalt chloride paper is blue when anhydrous and turns pink on exposure to water. What explains this colour change?",choices:["The ligands around the cobalt ion change from chloride ions to water molecules, changing the d-orbital splitting and hence the colour observed","Cobalt chloride paper changes colour due to a change in temperature alone, with no ligand change involved","The paper itself reacts chemically with water to form an entirely new compound containing no cobalt","Anhydrous cobalt chloride is colourless, and water is responsible for all of the colour observed"],explain:"As water molecules replace chloride ions as ligands around the cobalt ion, the change in ligand identity changes the d-orbital splitting and therefore the colour, from blue (anhydrous, Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> ligands) to pink (hydrated, H<sub>2</sub>O ligands) &mdash; a common test for the presence of water."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why is Sc<sup>3+</sup> colourless in all of its compounds?",choices:["Its d sublevel, 3d<sup>0</sup>, is completely empty, so there are no d electrons available to be promoted in a d&ndash;d transition","Its d sublevel is completely full, leaving no vacancy for an electron to be promoted into","Scandium is not a d-block element, so it has no d orbitals at all","Sc<sup>3+</sup> actually does absorb visible light, but only in the ultraviolet region"],explain:"Sc<sup>3+</sup> has an empty 3d<sup>0</sup> sublevel, so there are no d electrons to excite into the higher split level, meaning no d-d transition (and no visible light absorption) can occur."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: Why is Zn<sup>2+</sup> colourless in all of its compounds?",choices:["Its d sublevel, 3d<sup>10</sup>, is completely full, leaving no vacant orbital at the higher split level for an electron to be promoted into","Its d sublevel is completely empty, so there are no electrons available to absorb light","Zinc ions are always found only in the gas phase, where no colour can be observed","Zn<sup>2+</sup> actually is coloured, but only in extremely concentrated solutions"],explain:"Zn<sup>2+</sup> has a completely full 3d<sup>10</sup> sublevel, so there is no vacant d orbital for an electron to be promoted into, meaning no d-d transition can occur and the ion remains colourless."},
{ch:10,q:"HL: What general rule explains why both Sc<sup>3+</sup> (3d<sup>0</sup>) and Zn<sup>2+</sup> (3d<sup>10</sup>) are colourless, despite being at opposite ends of the d sublevel?",choices:["A d&ndash;d transition requires both at least one d electron to promote and at least one vacant d orbital to receive it; an empty or a completely full d sublevel rules this out in either direction","Only ions with an odd number of d electrons can ever be coloured","Colour in transition metal ions depends only on the overall charge of the ion, not on its electron configuration","Every d-block ion is coloured except for those in group 12"],explain:"Because a d-d transition needs both an electron to promote and a vacant orbital to receive it, an ion with either no d electrons (Sc<sup>3+</sup>) or a completely full d sublevel (Zn<sup>2+</sup>) cannot undergo one, so both are colourless."}
]
